[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Broken pipe produced the wrong exit status
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Symptom: with stdout connected to a pipe whose read end is closed, the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   program was killed by `SIGPIPE` (no exit code, signal 13, shell reports 141)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   while the Rust program printed nothing and exited 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Cause: the Rust standard library installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `main` runs. The failing `write` therefore surfaced as an `EPIPE` `io::Error`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   which the translation discards (matching C's ignored `printf` return value),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so the process ran to a normal `return 0`. A C program started from a shell
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   inherits the *default* `SIGPIPE` disposition instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Fix: `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` calls `signal(SIGPIPE,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   SIG_DFL)` as the first statement of `main`, before any output is produced.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Covered by: `broken_pipe_exit_status_matches`. Reverting the fix makes that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   test fail with `C (None, Some(13)) vs Rust (Some(0), None)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. stdin was slurped to EOF instead of being read lazily
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Symptom: given `"1 2 3 4\n"` on a pipe that is *not* then closed, the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   program printed `1 2 1 4` and exited 0 immediately; the Rust program hung
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   until stdin was closed (observed as `timeout` status 124).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Cause: the scanner was built with `std::io::stdin().read_to_end(&mut buf)`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so no conversion could start before end-of-input. `scanf` instead pulls bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   on demand and stops one byte past the digits it accepted, so the trailing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   newline is enough to finish the fourth conversion.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Fix: `Scanner` in `src/main.rs` is now generic over `Read`, holds a single
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   byte of lookahead (the byte `scanf` would have `ungetc`'d) and reads through a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `BufReader` only when `peek()` has nothing buffered. `EINTR` is retried; any
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   other read error becomes a sticky EOF, which is what a `FILE` error indicator
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   does to the following conversions.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Covered by: `stdin_held_open_does_not_block`, which feeds input in chunks and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   keeps the write end alive. Restoring the eager read makes it fail with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `driver did not finish within 5s while stdin stayed open`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. Dead duplicate scanner removed (not a behavioural mismatch)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `src/scanf.rs` contained a second, slightly different `scanf` emulation that was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m never declared as a module and therefore never compiled. It has been deleted so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that the only conversion logic in the crate is the one actually exercised.
